winebus: the lights a user chose, in the light changes a client already sends.

A DualSense has a lightbar and five small player LEDs, and on this route nobody
asks the user what they should show. In hid-203611.log, Mortal Shell 2 under
Steam Input on Bluetooth, 8 of 1858 client writes set a light, and every one
of them is Steam's; a ninth is Steam's 00/08 release-LEDs packet, which sets
none. At each config activation, and again on a reconnect, Steam sends player
0x24 with RGB 00ffff (flag1 0x14), RGB 00ffff alone (flag1 0x04), then player
0x00 with 00ffff (flag1 0x14). The Desktop activation is different: 00/08, then
player 0x00 with 00ffff (flag1 0x14), then 00ffff alone, and no 0x24. The game
itself sends no light at all.

THREE REGISTRY VALUES, REG_DWORD, under the pad's own key
HKLM\System\CurrentControlSet\Services\winebus\Devices\054c/0ce6 or 054c/0df2,
read as the device arrives, beside VibrationMode and before
dualsense_usb_emulation_fixups can replace the pid, so always under the real
model:

  LightbarColour   0x01RRGGBB replaces the colour; 0x01000000 is the lightbar
                   off. Anything without the 0x01 marker is pass-through.
  PlayerLights     0x100 | pattern replaces the player LEDs; 0x100 is off,
                   0x104 / 0x10A / 0x115 / 0x11B are players 1 to 4. Anything
                   else is pass-through.
  LightbarRelease  0 never, 1 (the default) a separate release packet, 2 the
                   release folded into the client's packet. See below.

The markers keep "black" and "off" apart from "nothing asked", which is the
lesson of mgvf-0022's all-zero sentinel.

WHAT CHANGES. Only CLIENT packets, at the three dispatch entry paths: the native
Bluetooth 0x31 after the ride and the vibration rewrite, the wired 0x02, and the
emulated 0x02 on a copy at the dispatch arm -- never inside
dualsense_usb_set_output_report, which the haptics thread also calls. The
dispatch conditions now let a light packet reach those paths when vibration and
the ride are off. In each packet only a field whose enable bit the client set is
replaced: the colour when flag1 0x04 is set, the pattern when flag1 0x10 is set,
keeping the client's instant bit 0x20 (Steam and SDL send 0x24). No flag bit is
ever set or cleared. A Bluetooth packet is signed again only when a byte
changed; a wired 0x02 is rewritten only when it is 48 to 64 bytes, long enough
to carry the whole lightbar. Nothing is kept but one claim per arrival, no
owner is guessed at, and no packet of ours is ever read back.

THE RELEASE, AND WHY IT IS AN OPTION. Linux hid-playstation says a Bluetooth
DualSense keeps its startup colour and ignores lightbar programming until it has
been sent a lightbar setup release (flag2 0x02, common byte 41 = 0x02, "light
out"). Steam never sends one: flag2 bit 0x02 is clear in every packet of
hid-203611.log. The macOS logs of the same evening show macOS's own colour
writes carrying the setup bits and Steam's cyan reaching the radio intact with
none. So with LightbarColour set, on Bluetooth, before the first client lightbar
packet after the device arrives, LightbarRelease 1 sends one release-only report
of ours from that same client write (a signed 31 00 10 packet natively, the 0x02
form through the translation when emulated) and then the client's rewritten
packet; 2 sets the same two fields in that client packet instead and adds
nothing. Once per arrival: the claim is taken before the write, so a failed
release is not retried until the pad next arrives, and its WARN comes at most
once per arrival. Client writes are already serialised by the device lock.
WHETHER THE PAD NEEDS THE RELEASE AT ALL, AND WHETHER FORM 2 WORKS TOO, IS NOT
MEASURED. That is what the value is for. Form 1 was measured on 2026-09-14: on
a DualSense Edge on Bluetooth under Steam Input the release went out with status
0, Steam's light packets were rewritten, and the chosen colour and player light
held in two titles (hid-090147.log).

NOT IN THIS PATCH. No packet at IRP_MN_START_DEVICE and no new feature kick: a
pad that no client ever writes a light to -- a pure XInput title with no Steam
Input -- gets nothing yet. Brightness, the mic LED and Steam's release-LEDs
packet are passed through untouched. Values are keyed by model, so every pad of
one model in a bottle gets the same lights, and the player number is the user's:
the xinput slot cannot be seen from here.

TRACES (+hid): "lights will be set" once per arrival with the decoded values;
"set the client's lights (Bluetooth|cable|emulated)" for each packet a byte of
which changed; "released the lightbar before the first colour" with the form
and the write's status, and a WARN if that write fails.

The pure half -- decoding, rewrite, release builders -- is in dualsense_usb.c
and covered by usb_emu_test.c against the captured packets: Steam's trio with
only the expected bytes changed and a CRC that agrees with an independent
bitwise CRC-32, the inert, SDL, 00/08 and haptics-thread packets unchanged,
libScePad's open keeping its setup byte and flags, the 47/48/64-byte bounds, the
release packet on both envelopes, and the fold.

